experiment: baseprobe
  instrument        baseprobe   trial seed 0000000001359d97   9714 trials per session, sampled at 16 checkpoints
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  question          boundprobe's phase 2 -- aligned and outside growing at
                    the same exponent with gain frozen -- is node perturbation
                    on its variance floor. Is that floor the RULE's, or the
                    one-bit-per-trial CRITERION's?
  the gate          phase-2 gain slope. Rising on a graded arm means the
                    criterion was the limit; flat on every arm means the rule
                    is, and 137 Hz is this architecture's number.
  the confound      graded is divided by the creature's own mean absolute
                    deviation, so E|reward| = |praise| by construction. The
                    realised mean is printed; >15% off is CONFOUNDED, not read.

  arm        |reward|    praise      aligned exp 1/2  outside exp 1/2  gain 1->16  dF1 last (Hz)
  binary     0.500       0.544       0.82 / 0.26      0.40 / 0.31      1.37 -> 3.35   138.8 +/- 11.5
  graded     0.506       0.538       0.63 / 0.57      0.41 / 0.43      1.62 -> 3.16   91.7 +/- 28.1
  graded-1   0.499       0.544       0.90 / 0.68      0.45 / 0.45      0.99 -> 2.76   114.9 +/- 34.8

  PHASE 2 IS THE QUESTION. Its signature is aligned and outside growing
  at the SAME exponent -- separation zero is the floor:
  binary     aligned 0.26  outside 0.31  separation -0.05 +/- 0.13 (-0.4 SE)  final aligned 0.06060
  graded     aligned 0.57  outside 0.43  separation +0.14 +/- 0.15 (0.9 SE)  final aligned 0.07014
  graded-1   aligned 0.68  outside 0.45  separation +0.24 +/- 0.18 (1.3 SE)  final aligned 0.07134

  THE FLOOR IS THE RULE'S, NOT THE CRITERION'S. No graded arm keeps the
  aligned component outgrowing the useless ones into phase 2 at 2 SE, and
  none separates from the binary arm at 2 SE. Handing node perturbation
  the magnitude it was discarding does not move its variance floor, which
  is what Hiratani's analysis says should happen: the floor is set by the
  perturbation noise the estimator injects, not by the resolution of the
  signal it reads. 137 Hz is this architecture's number.
